## Why This Is Asked
Streaming frequency sketches are common in prod telemetry. This question probes knowledge of CMS, accuracy guarantees, batch processing, and amortized space-time trade-offs.

## Key Concepts
- Count-Min Sketch (CMS)
- ε, δ error bounds
- Update/query complexity for batches
- Space complexity O(wd)
- Trade-offs vs exact counting

## Code Example
```javascript
// CMS skeleton
const w = Math.ceil(Math.E / eps);
const d = Math.ceil(Math.log(1/delta));
const table = Array.from({length: d}, () => Array(w).fill(0));

function update(x) { for (let i = 0; i < d; i++) table[i][hash_i(i, x) % w]++; }

function query(x) { let m = Infinity; for (let i = 0; i < d; i++) m = Math.min(m, table[i][hash_i(i, x) % w]); return m; }
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would deletions or non-monotone streams affect CMS choices?
- How would you monitor actual error in production without degrading performance?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to reason about fixed-memory distinct counting in streaming data, a common real-world need for analytics in constrained environments.

## Key Concepts
- HyperLogLog for cardinality estimation
- Mergeable sketches across batches
- Relative error ~ 1.04/√m; memory fixed by m

## Code Example
```javascript
function hllAdd(registers, x, hash) {
  const m = registers.length;
  const i = hash & (m - 1);
  const w = hash >>> Math.log2(m);
  const rank = leadingZeros(w) + 1;
  registers[i] = Math.max(registers[i], rank);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you choose p in a live system with memory constraints?
- How would you explain the trade-offs of using HLL vs a linear-counting approach in presence of enormous M?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design simple online statistics with fixed small memory. Mode is a practical KPI for signal quality in routing decisions on ride-hailing platforms. It requires maintaining counts and batch updates.

## Key Concepts

- Online counters on a small alphabet
- Tie-breaking and stability
- Time/memory trade-offs in streaming

## Code Example

```javascript
function OnlineMode() {
  const freq = [0,0,0,0,0,0]; // 1..5
  let mode = 1, modeCount = 0;
  return {
    update(batch){
      for (const r of batch){
        freq[r]++;
        if (freq[r] > modeCount || (freq[r] === modeCount && r < mode)){
          mode = r; modeCount = freq[r];
        }
      }
      return {mode, modeCount};
    }
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt for a larger rating scale (e.g., 1–1000)?
- How would you support sliding-window mode across the last K batches?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests streaming algorithm intuition and amortized reasoning. Detecting heavy hitters with bounded memory on a growing stream is a common interview topic; Misra–Gries provides deterministic guarantees with simple updates.

## Key Concepts
- Misra–Gries heavy-hitters
- Choice of k = floor(1/phi) + 1 to bound missed items
- Amortized O(1) per element, O(k) memory
- Trade-offs: larger k reduces false positives but uses more memory

## Code Example
```javascript
class MGHeavyHitters {
  constructor(k) {
    this.k = k;
    this.counters = new Map();
  }
  update(x) {
    if (this.counters.has(x)) {
      this.counters.set(x, this.counters.get(x) + 1);
      return;
    }
    if (this.counters.size < this.k) {
      this.counters.set(x, 1);
      return;
    }
    for (const [key, val] of Array.from(this.counters)) {
      if (val === 1) this.counters.delete(key);
      else this.counters.set(key, val - 1);
    }
  }
  candidates() { return Array.from(this.counters.keys()); }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you validate the phi-bound with a second pass or a secondary sketch in production?
- How does batch size B influence practical update throughput and memory usage?

## Why This Is Asked
Realistic scenario: social/ride networks grow incrementally; counting triangles relates to clustering.

## Key Concepts
- Incremental triangle counting; degree-based orientation; amortized analysis; sparse graphs; memory efficiency.

## Code Example
```javascript
function addBatch(G, batch){
  // G stores oriented adj lists and degree
  for(const [u,v] of batch){
    if(G.deg[u] > G.deg[v] || (G.deg[u] === G.deg[v] && u > v)) swap(u,v);
    // ensure oriented edge u->v
    G.adj[u].push(v);
    const common = intersect(G.adj[u], G.out[v]);
    G.triangles += common.length;
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt for dynamic edge deletions?
- What are practical bounds in real-world m,n values?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design incremental spectral methods for dynamic graphs and reason about trade-offs between update time, accuracy, and memory.

## Key Concepts

- Eigenvector centrality; Rayleigh quotient
- Low-rank updates; batch dynamics
- Subspace iteration; spectral gap
- Complexity trade-offs; memory vs accuracy

## Code Example

```javascript
// simplified incremental update
function updateCentrality(X, A, DeltaA, touched) {
  const Y = project(DeltaA, X);
  X = orthonormalize(X.add(DeltaA.dot(X)));
  return X;
}
```

